orld data. When a model's behavior shifts under novel conditions, a zero-knowledge proof of training compliance provides zero protection against emergent failures. Regulators must have the authority to inspect model weights and training data to identify systemic biases before deployment. What is the rollback plan when an uninspectable model triggers a systemic financial run, and who has the authority to trigger it?\n\nPolicymakers must place strict product liability on upstream developers because downstream operators cannot patch emergent design defects. In the financial sector, the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 holds corporate executives personally liable for accounting failures to protect the public from systemic collapse. Similarly, the Department of Justice must hold foundational model developers liable for systemic harms, including the unauthorized aggregation of relational data across applications. Downstream users bear the brunt of identity exposure, while upstream developers pocket the profits from unverified deployments. Placing liability on developers forces them to internalize the costs of safety, aligning financial incentives with public stability.",
