cks directly into the runtime environment, we can mathematically prove that an AI agent complied with specific privacy and safety parameters during a transaction. This mechanism replaces slow, discretionary regulatory reviews with real-time, automated verification. This setup benefits the public through absolute privacy guarantees and benefits developers by protecting their core intellectual property. Security-focused agencies and open-source advocates support this approach, while legacy regulators oppose it because it reduces their discretionary power. This mirrors the historical battle over end-to-end encryption in the 1990s. Authorities claimed encryption would shield criminals. Instead, it became the foundational security architecture of the global internet.\n\nDownstream allocation of algorithmic liability, paired with cryptographic verification, is the only way to sustain rapid deployment while securing accountability. When we choose to freeze development under the weight of ex-ante licensing or class-action litigation, we are choosing to let people suffer from diseases we could automate a cure for. The cost of delay is measured in human lives, not just dollars. By shifting liability to the specific downstream actions of agents—using negligence standards to evaluate actual harm rather than penalizing the training phase—we keep the path open for permissionless innovation. Cryptographic protocols automate compliance checks, allowing us to audit agent behavior dynamically without stalling the deployment of life-saving agentic networks. If we allow legacy legal frameworks to choke this infrastructure, we cede our technological edge to international competitors who do not share our values. The United States cannot afford to stall the $500 billion infrastructure investment announced to secure national competitiveness, as highlighted in the 'Eight ways AI will shape geopolitics in 2026   Atlantic Council' report. The choice is clear: we can govern through mathematical certainty and targeted liability, or we can let regulatory paralysis dictate our future.",
