  "statement": "Existing privacy frameworks are fundamentally broken because they treat data as an individual transaction rather than a relational, network-level asset. Let us be clear about the technical reality: exact model unlearning is a computational fantasy. The Safetyist is right that we cannot mathematically prove a model has forgotten a specific training input. But upstream strict liability turns this technical limit into a moat. It protects the seven dominant tech companies that hold AI capabilities far exceeding all other institutions. It turns compliance into a luxury only they can afford. Meanwhile, the Accelerationist's push to shift liability to downstream deployers via contracts is a classic corporate shell game. It leaves local businesses holding the bag when autonomous agents leak customer data.\n\nIndividual consent cannot protect you when your neighbor's data reveals your own private habits. Relational privacy requires collective defense, not individual tick-boxes. Policymakers must establish collective fiduciary data trusts. These trusts must hold legal title to citizen data. They must negotiate access terms on behalf of entire communities. We must back this with a federal statutory framework. This framework must define unauthorized agent training as a per se intrusion upon seclusion. It must carry statutory liquidated damages of $1,000 per violation. This bypasses the regulatory delays of the Federal Trade Commission. It puts real enforcement power directly into the hands of citizens.\n\nThe Safetyist wants to dodge regulatory capture by using decentralized auditing consortia. This is a dangerous historical repeating loop. Look at the 2008 financial crisis. Private credit rating agencies stamped toxic mortgage-backed securities as AAA because the banks paid their fees. Private AI auditors will do the exact same thing. They will sell compliance stamps to Google and Microsoft while shielding them from liability. These consortia will become strategic islands of ignorance. They will protect corporate giants from lawsuits while freezing out open-source developers who cannot pay the audit fees. Who actually pays when these private cartels fail and a major agent network leaks the healthcare records of millions of people?",
